cated guidance specifically  for environmental monitoring of clean  rooms  in vaccine manufacturing facilities  now as i mentioned earlier both the  european gmp and the north american cgmp  documents  tell the reader to refer to the  classification procedures laid down in  iso  1464 when they are classifying their  clean room  now the iso standard is not a gmp  document  and it's actually really designed to  give guidance rather than mandate  but the fact that it is referenced by  the gmps gives it the  same status as the gmp document for  those manufacturers regulated by gmp  now this standard has a wide range of  clean room classes as you can see from  one to nine  and also covers a wide range of particle  sizes from 0.1 micron to 5 microns  and you can see in the middle of the  chart there the number of particles per  cubic meter  for each class which is allowed  now the idea is from the iso standard  that the user must decide  what particle sizes they're interested  in and how clean the clean room must be  for their process and then they choose a  subset  of the clean room class and particle  size from the iso table  now gmp has decided that the particle  size or interest  are 0.5 and 5 microns  and you can see that the grade b at rest  the number of particles match iso 5.  there's your 0.5 and 5 and there's the  number of particles  so you can see gmp has taken a subset of  the iso standard  an important thing to note and a very  contentious issue  is that the 2015 version of the iso  standard 1464-1  does not specify the number of particles  at five mic  icons for iso class 5. there's no number  given  however regulated clean rooms need to  comply with the gmp rules not the iso  rules  so if gmp says they are required to  measure 0.5  and 5 microns so they have to do that so  the fact that the iso standard no longer  mentions the five microns  used to but it doesn't now it does not  mean that the gmp clean room  users do not have to measurement they  still do