t each site using portable  air particle counters  typically there are many manual sop  steps to be followed in  in the routine environmental monitoring  program and the first of these is the  technician firstly has to make sure  they're using the correct version of the  environment of their environmental  monitoring sop  sometimes they change and you need to  make sure you've got the right one and  of course they're paper  based so it's you need to make sure  you're using the correct  sop and then they have to follow the  sampling map  defined in the sop and go to each sample  location marked on a sampling map  and then they have to manually type the  location name for the sample  into the counter via the on-screen  keyboard  and next they have to configure the  counter to sample for the correct amount  of time  and for the correct number of samples  according to the sop  and this may vary for different  locations  having taken the sample the next thing  they do is print the sample results out  from the counter  and handwrite comments onto the print  out and sign it  oh with their name and by signing it  they're making it attributable  attributable to themselves and at the  end of each day  they photocopy all the printouts and  then they manually transcribe  the results from each and every piece of  paper  into an electronic record  remember there's typically hundreds of  these per day and thousands per month  and finally the electronic record is  reviewed by the supervisor  to ensure that all the samples have been  taken and that the data in the  electronic records  match the paper printouts so they have  to manually check  that the electronic record matches the  paper printouts  now you can see this is a very manual  process and with thousands of sample  pieces of paper manual data  transcriptions every month  sadly the opportunity for human error to  creep in and impact the integrity of the  data  is extremely high  now in their 2018 frequently asked  questions 